Subject: link2symlink: name /proc/self/exe after the faked hard link

On Linux, /proc/self/exe of a program started through a hard link is that
link's own path. link2symlink fakes a hard link as a symlink chain into its
l2s directory, so PRoot reported the l2s backing file instead
(".../.l2s.coreutils.dpkg-new0001.0116"), a name no tracee ever used.

Ubuntu 26.04's rust-coreutils is a multicall binary installed as hard links
(/usr/lib/cargo/bin/coreutils/<util>). Ubuntu patches it
(require-utility-to-be-invoked-at-matching-path.patch) to refuse to run
unless basename(/proc/self/exe) equals the utility requested. After the
0.10.0-1ubuntu2~26.04.1 update re-created those links under PRoot, every
coreutils command failed with "Security violation", and so did sudo's
postinst.

At execve, when the executed file lives in the l2s directory, walk the path
the tracee passed: follow ordinary symlinks as the kernel does, and stop at
the faked hard link that leads to the executed file. Report that link as the
new /proc/self/exe. Only the directory of each step is translated: the
extension's own translated_path() hook swaps a faked hard link for its
backing file and would hide the link being looked for. Nothing changes when
no faked hard link is involved. This is generic (any hard-linked
executable), not package-specific.

Tested: tests/proot-hardlink-identity/host-test.sh (native build; five of
its checks fail without this patch) and device/device-test.sh (clean
ubuntu-base 26.04.1 -> full-upgrade to rust-coreutils 0.10.0, 18/18).

Upstream: termux/proot @ 7266fb3e8516535682f5a9c8f3a7e70f6506eddb (v5.1.107.92)

